WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:09.400
So far, we have seen different algorithms
for read mapping.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.119
The next question that we are going to ask
is whether we can model a particular DNA sequence

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:18.730
and we don't know anything about DNA to predict
what it is and what is its function base on

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.700
certain aspects and certain models.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.630
For example, if we have a new sequence of
DNA, what can we do to find out for information

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.720
about the sequence.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.910
One option is to align it so that we can do
a database search and infer what have already

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.030
been known about the similar sequence.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:40.109
Another option is to visualize it so we can
inspect the content of the DNA whether it

00:00:40.109 --> 00:00:45.309
has a nucleotide composition that is very
different from what we are supposed to see?

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:50.370
Does it have certain k-mers which are existing
in higher frequencies than the others?

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:54.910
Is there any recurrent pattern that we observed
in the DNA and so on.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.949
The third option is to model the particular
sequence.

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:03.800
Modeling refer to the situation where we can
make some hypothesis about the sequence.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.830
For example, are there any common ancestor
for the sequence?

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:12.760
Are there any particular functionality associated
with certain regions of the sequence and so

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:14.030
on.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:18.670
By modelling, we are trying to build a generative
model which is a mathematical model that will

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:23.170
allow us to predict different functionalities
of this DNA.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:28.350
Given a long sequence of DNA, different regions
of the DNA play a different function.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:34.810
For example, some part of the DNA can be an
exon, intron, promoter region, CpG island

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.560
and etc.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.990
By building a generative model, we will be
able to identify and annotate these regions

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:44.240
given a new piece of unlabeled DNA.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.640
Of note, the generative model must be able
to infer the functionality of non-identical

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.850
sequences and recognize the pattern of known
sequence.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.910
Once the model is developed, we can classify
the non-annotated sequences based on the annotated

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.460
regions.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.070
These generative models are often generated
by probabilistic modelling.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:09.509
Why is it useful to have a probabilistic model
for the biological sequences?

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:13.799
First of all, biological data is often times
noisy.

00:02:13.799 --> 00:02:18.720
Data collected from different sources may
give rise to various variations due to biologically

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.540
variability.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.340
The probabilistic modeling techniques can
be very useful in updating our previous knowledge

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.250
that we already have about the biological
sequences.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:34.819
When we are using probabilistic sequence modelling,
we are not limited to yes or no kind of answers.

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:39.689
Instead, it generates answer together with
the level of confidence.

00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:44.099
One of the probabilistic sequence modeling
that is commonly used is called the Markov

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:45.239
models.

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:49.739
There are classic Markov chains and there
is also extended models for Markov chains

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:52.409
which are called hidden Markov models.

00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:56.930
A classic example of a hidden Markov model
is shown here.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:01.379
In this model, we have three different nodes,
represented in circles.

00:03:01.379 --> 00:03:06.609
The arrow marks from one node to another node
are called transitions or edges.

00:03:06.609 --> 00:03:12.299
In this example, it is a hidden Markov model
to describe the weather of a particular day.

00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:17.190
We see that there are different transitions
between sunny whether to cloudy to raining.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:22.660
For example, a transition from sunny to being
cloudy is 0.15 meaning that if a particular

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.780
day is sunny the probability that the next
day will be cloudy is 15%.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.209
The hidden Markov models are applied in different
biological sequence analysis.

00:03:33.209 --> 00:03:37.909
For example, hidden Markov models have been
used for predicting genes.

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.709
If we have a new sequence and we want to identify
the genes inside that sequence, we can use

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:47.689
hidden Markov models to annotate the regions
first and then identify the location of a

00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:48.689
gene.

00:03:48.689 --> 00:03:53.450
Hidden Markov models can provide very good
genetic models for performing different dynamic

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:58.469
alignment algorithms for pairwise as well
as multiple sequence alignment.

00:03:58.469 --> 00:04:04.090
We can also use hidden Markov models for building
a profile for a sequence family, base calling,

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:09.409
determining DNA sequencing errors, prediction
of the secondary structure of protein, identifying

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:12.650
copy number variations and many more.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:17.329
In other words, hidden Markov models are very
important mathematical models that have been

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:20.720
applied in various biological sequence analysis.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.450
Here, we are going to look at a very simple
example of how we can use Markov chain to

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.220
model the CpG islands.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.880
In the genome, the frequency of CG dinucleotide
occurs in low frequency.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.530
For example, in the human genome the GC content
is about 42%.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:45.450
So, we should observe G and C with probability
of 21% in frequency for each of them.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:50.780
If we consider this as the expected frequency
of the bases G and C, then the expected frequency

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:56.170
that we should observe the CG dinucleotide
CG is about 4.41%.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.120
But in reality, we observed only about 1%.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:05.540
What is the reason that we see this type of
lower frequency of CG dinucleotide?

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:10.980
This is because naturally cytosine is chemically
methylated to methyl cytosine and methyl cytosine

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.830
is easily deaminated and converted into thymine.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:19.320
The C to T conversion is very naturally occurring
in the DNA.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:25.350
As a result, the observed frequency of CG
is much lower than the expected frequency.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:30.880
The methylated CpG residues are often associated
with gene regulation at the promoter and exon

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:31.950
regions.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:36.440
However, it is very interesting to note that
there are some regions where this methylation

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.780
process is suppressed, particularly in specific
regions near the promoter or the transcriptional

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.390
start site of many genes.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:49.730
These regions where methylation process is
suppressed are called CpG Islands.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:55.390
In the CpG Islands, the GC dinucleotide appears
relatively more frequent compared to the rest

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:56.880
of the genome.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:03.210
So, identifying the CPG Island can be very
useful in identifying different genes.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.400
Here is the problem.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.580
Given a short stretch of genomic sequence,
can we decide whether this stretch of genomic

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:13.180
sequence come from a CpG Island or not.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.580
To solve this problem, we need a model that
generates sequences in which the probability

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.700
of a symbol depends on the previous symbol.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:25.770
Here is a graph structured model and the Markov
chain model for the DNA.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:30.860
The graph shows 4 different nodes: A, T, G,
and C. Within these nodes there are certain

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:35.220
transitions which denotes the probability
of a particular nucleotide following another

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.770
particular nucleotide.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:43.580
By calculating the transition probability
of each node from known CpG sequence vs non-CpG

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:48.480
sequence from the dataset, we can construct
the table of transition probabilities as shown

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.930
here.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:54.880
For example, in the “+” or CpG-island
dataset, the transition probability from A

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.920
to A is 0.18, A to C is 0.274 and so forth.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:07.520
It is interesting to note that, the probability
of CG is higher in the CpG model vs the non-CpG

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:08.740
model.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:14.510
This is expected as the frequency of observing
the CG dinucleotides in CpG island is much

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:16.090
higher.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.860
Once we have obtained these models for discrimination,
these can be used to compute the log odds

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:25.500
ratio by calculating the log of the ratio
of the probabilities for the plus model and

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.390
the minus model.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:32.170
In the table here, we show the beta values
which are represented as bits.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:36.550
We can also plot the data where the x-axis
represents the bits, and we can can see two

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:38.500
different areas.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:43.460
The sequences the dark gray represent the
CpG Islands whereas the lighter gray areas

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.710
correspond to the other regions.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:51.200
Thus, we can use two different Markov chain
models to identify whether a particular sequence

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:53.400
came from CpG Island or not.

